WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.733 --> 00:00:02.202
-"SNL" 40th --

00:00:02.202 --> 00:00:05.271
they used all our dressing rooms
for fancy people.

00:00:05.271 --> 00:00:08.474
Taylor Swift was in here,
and she very sweetly

00:00:08.474 --> 00:00:10.310
left a thank-you note,
and I was like,

00:00:10.310 --> 00:00:12.145
"Yeah, this is my life now."

00:00:12.145 --> 00:00:13.713
Which I still do
all the time.

00:00:13.713 --> 00:00:16.416
But mostly my life is, like,
picking up my dog [bleep]

00:00:16.416 --> 00:00:19.085
and stuff like that,
you know.

00:00:19.085 --> 00:00:24.256
♪♪

00:00:24.256 --> 00:00:26.925
-In my first season,
I shared a dressing room

00:00:26.925 --> 00:00:28.327
with Kate and Cecily.

00:00:28.327 --> 00:00:30.196
We were right next to
the women's restroom,

00:00:30.196 --> 00:00:31.831
which I always thought
was a big win,

00:00:31.831 --> 00:00:33.399
'cause I'm a dumper.

00:00:33.399 --> 00:00:36.403
Let's see. Is this going good?
[ Laughs ] Um...

00:00:36.403 --> 00:00:39.405
Our first year, I do feel like
our dressing room kind of became

00:00:39.405 --> 00:00:42.007
like the hub
for all the new cast

00:00:42.007 --> 00:00:43.609
and new writers, so, like,

00:00:43.609 --> 00:00:46.979 line:0%
Timmy Robinson and Chris Kelly
and Sarah Schneider.

00:00:46.979 --> 00:00:48.981
You know, I mean, like,
it's also like, you're in there

00:00:48.981 --> 00:00:50.383
for hours and hours
at a time,

00:00:50.383 --> 00:00:52.117
especially in your
first seasons

00:00:52.117 --> 00:00:53.653
'cause you're not in that much.

00:00:53.653 --> 00:00:56.188
It is, like, where
the three of us really bonded,

00:00:56.188 --> 00:00:57.824
which is very nice.

00:00:57.824 --> 00:01:01.528
My second season, Cecily and I
shared a dressing room,

00:01:01.528 --> 00:01:03.462
and then the following season
I got

00:01:03.462 --> 00:01:06.098
the beautiful digs
that I'm in now.

00:01:06.098 --> 00:01:07.500
You know, no windows.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:09.101
They actually just redid
the carpet.

00:01:09.101 --> 00:01:11.471
Gave us new couches 'cause they
were eaten by moths.

00:01:11.471 --> 00:01:13.472
So I'm living the high life.

00:01:13.472 --> 00:01:15.574
You know, we're in these rooms,
like, all the time.

00:01:15.574 --> 00:01:18.477
I've tried to just surround
myself with little things

00:01:18.477 --> 00:01:19.913
that just, like, make me happy.

00:01:19.913 --> 00:01:21.881
Almost everything in here
is like some little

00:01:21.881 --> 00:01:24.216
reminder of a person
either I worked with

00:01:24.216 --> 00:01:26.452
or something that someone
gave to me.

00:01:26.452 --> 00:01:30.156
So this does become like
my home three days of the week.

00:01:30.156 --> 00:01:34.627
I have a framed photo
that Colin gave me

00:01:34.627 --> 00:01:39.232
that is my husband I giving the
finger in the back of a taxi.

00:01:39.232 --> 00:01:40.900
I really like that one.

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:43.703
Truly, like, some of my darkest
times have happened in here,

00:01:43.703 --> 00:01:46.405
which is why I have so many,
like, lotions and potions

00:01:46.405 --> 00:01:48.441
for, like, camouflaging.

00:01:48.441 --> 00:01:51.010
But it's like, you know,
we work these insane hours.

00:01:51.010 --> 00:01:53.846
At the end of it you still
have to come back here usually

00:01:53.846 --> 00:01:57.049
and keep going, and so
I mostly use this space

00:01:57.049 --> 00:01:59.919
to, like, recoup in some way.

00:01:59.919 --> 00:02:03.055 line:0%
-Featuring Aidy Bryant.

00:02:03.055 --> 00:02:04.590 line:0%
-My first season,

00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:07.327
I hosted a presidential debate,

00:02:07.327 --> 00:02:10.396
and it was maybe like
my fifth show ever.

00:02:10.396 --> 00:02:12.632
It was this weird scenario where

00:02:12.632 --> 00:02:14.500
I couldn't have cue cards
in front of me.

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:17.536
They had cue cards guys
dressed up as extras,

00:02:17.536 --> 00:02:19.238 line:0%
and then they would pop up
a single card

00:02:19.238 --> 00:02:20.973 line:0%
and then pop it back down.

00:02:20.973 --> 00:02:22.374 line:0%
And I was so nervous.

00:02:22.374 --> 00:02:24.577 line:0%
I remember just over
and over again

00:02:24.577 --> 00:02:26.245 line:0%
in my dressing room going, like,

00:02:26.245 --> 00:02:29.148
"Good evening.
Good evening."

00:02:29.148 --> 00:02:30.683
Full psycho.

00:02:30.683 --> 00:02:32.218
Couldn't stop practicing that

00:02:32.218 --> 00:02:33.653
'cause I was so nervous.

00:02:33.653 --> 00:02:36.288
I still, to this day,
am, like, haunted by that

00:02:36.288 --> 00:02:37.824
first line where I'm like,

00:02:37.824 --> 00:02:40.259
"Good evening from
Hofstra University

00:02:40.259 --> 00:02:41.928
in Hempstead, New York."

00:02:41.928 --> 00:02:44.831
Right before air, a note was
sent down, I think from Lorne,

00:02:44.831 --> 00:02:46.733
that they were like,
"She looks too young.

00:02:46.733 --> 00:02:48.034
Add age makeup."

00:02:48.034 --> 00:02:49.802
So they were literally
counting down like,

00:02:49.802 --> 00:02:51.170
"5, 4, 3..."

00:02:51.170 --> 00:02:53.773
The makeup artist was, like,
desperately trying to put,

00:02:53.773 --> 00:02:55.975
like, shadow under my eyes
and stuff.

00:02:55.975 --> 00:02:58.277
He didn't mean to, but he
poked me in the eye.

00:02:58.277 --> 00:03:01.114
And they were like,
"3, 2, 1."

00:03:01.114 --> 00:03:03.049 line:0%
[ Laughter ]
Good evening from

00:03:03.049 --> 00:03:05.751 line:0%
Hofstra University
in Hempstead, New York.

00:03:05.751 --> 00:03:09.255 line:0%
I'm Candy Crowley from
CNN's "State of the Union."

00:03:09.255 --> 00:03:11.090 line:0%
[ Applause ]
But it worked out okay,

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:13.493
and, like, I got to say
"Live from New York" --

00:03:13.493 --> 00:03:16.095
the very first time I ever
got to say "Live from New York."

00:03:16.095 --> 00:03:20.933
Thank you, and live from
New York, it's "Saturday Night"!

00:03:20.933 --> 00:03:23.536
Tom Hanks was also
in the sketch, and Bill Hader,

00:03:23.536 --> 00:03:25.705
and they both, like,
came on either side of me

00:03:25.705 --> 00:03:27.407
and, like, ran me down the hall,

00:03:27.407 --> 00:03:29.209
and all the hair and makeup
and costumes people

00:03:29.209 --> 00:03:30.542
were there, and they
clapped for me,

00:03:30.542 --> 00:03:32.211
and it was, like, one of
the coolest moments

00:03:32.211 --> 00:03:34.079
I've ever had here.

00:03:34.079 --> 00:03:37.950
I think of my office as, like,
a place for collaboration

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:40.686
and people to come by and us
to, like, write together,

00:03:40.686 --> 00:03:43.589
and I think of this as, like,
a much more quiet space.

00:03:43.589 --> 00:03:45.824
If people come in here,
it's usually to, like,

00:03:45.824 --> 00:03:48.494
talk about how nervous we are.
-..."Saturday Night"!

00:03:48.494 --> 00:03:50.363
-Every time a sketch ends,
you have to run

00:03:50.363 --> 00:03:51.730
and someone's pulling
your pants down

00:03:51.730 --> 00:03:53.266
and you're ripping
your shirt off,

00:03:53.266 --> 00:03:54.734
and this is like one of
the few places

00:03:54.734 --> 00:03:57.036
that we get to, like,
find a little quiet,

00:03:57.036 --> 00:04:01.240
and I think it's nice
to have a nice little cozy home.

00:04:01.240 --> 00:04:04.843
♪ And that's what I have here ♪

00:04:04.843 --> 00:04:06.246
[ Laughs ]

